Dictation

The learning value of "write code" isn't the typing — it's committing to a prediction and getting corrected. Typing is just the delivery mechanism, and it's the replaceable part. Voice-native versions:

Predict-then-run. You dictate a prediction in prose: "if the resume guard used <foo> instead of <bar>, <component>_test.go fails on the republished gate." I make the change and run it. You're right or wrong in under a minute. This is what your plumbing traces are already reaching for — but with a verdict at the end instead of a conclusion you can't check.

Break-and-read. I introduce a deliberate error; you read the compiler or test output and say what it means and which layer it's in. Go's compiler and go vet are unusually good teachers, and this is pure reading + speaking.

Spec-then-diff. You dictate what a function must do at spec level; I write it; you review the diff against your own spec. This is different from reviewing someone's MR: the intent is yours, so a mismatch tells you specifically where your model was wrong.

Also — don't write off editing entirely. "Rename fooNode to foo throughout <file>.go" dictates fine. It's greenfield 50-line functions that don't.

Tracing

You're not tracing too much. But you're entering from the expensive end.

Your notes trace forward a value across all 6 layers and makes you re-derive the wiring each time. Go gives you two cheaper entry points, and they're worth learning once as language-culture facts:

1. Interfaces are declared by the consumer. internal/<service>/<deps>.go declares <StoreIface> and <ExecIface> — the service states what it needs, and the concrete store type / client happen to satisfy it. There's no implements keyword to grep for. So "what can the service do to the database?" is answered by reading <deps>.go, not the store. And that file is heavily commented, including why status writes are deliberately excluded from <StoreIface>. That's exactly the local abstraction you're asking about, and here it's honest.

2. The wiring lives in main(). No DI container, no annotations, no framework magic. cmd/<binary>/main.go's run() is a flat, linear construction of the whole system, with comments explaining the choices ("Wiring a real notifier is a change to this line alone"). Four main.go files, ~560 lines total — that is the architecture diagram.

So the concrete swap: read <deps>.go plus the four main.gos once, deliberately, as a single exercise. After that the boundaries become trustworthy and you can stop at them.

When to keep tracing anyway: when a boundary doesn't tell you enough, that's usually a finding about the code, not about you. Your <id-propagation> note crossed six layers and concluded an argument is unused and the signature should be folded — that trace was justified, because the signature was lying. You already write a "What should be refactored" section; trust it more. Difficulty tracing is diagnostic, not a verdict on your Go.

And to answer the last part directly: consumer-side interfaces, internal/, cmd/, explicit struct-field DI, compile-time guards like var _ <ExecIface> = (*<pkg>.<struct>)(nil) — that's mainstream idiomatic Go done carefully, not <project> being idiosyncratic. What you learn from this layout transfers.



