Four hours makes sense, and I don't think it was your Go. Here's how I'd have run it.

Name the shape, get a checklist

This is a thread-one-field-end-to-end change — the most common shape there is. Naming it converts open-ended tracing into four questions:

- Is it minted at exactly one origin? (yes — caller mints, a dedicated commit validates the UUID)
- Does it survive every hop unmodified?
- What does absent mean? ("", and the migration tells you)
- Is it written atomically with what it describes? (yes, SetStatus)

You trace to check an invariant, not to understand. Much shorter trips, one hop at a time, no need to hold the stack in your head.

